## Summary

I wrote `tests/test_civic_properties.py` with three Hypothesis property tests driving the real engine through `CivicOffice` (simulated systems, simulated tier-1 rail, no network). Hypothesis is a dev-only dep: the import is wrapped in try/except, the module skips cleanly when missing (like `HAS_TEMPORAL`), and `hypothesis>=6.0` was added to `[project.optional-dependencies] dev` in `pyproject.toml`. Each example uses a fresh temp dir and a fixed, injected clock; `max_examples=60, deadline=None`. Full run: ~6s, well under 60s.

Command run: `python -X utf8 -m unittest tests.test_civic_properties -v` → `OK (expected failures=1)`. The existing suite (`tests/test_civic_case_actions.py`, 7 tests) still passes.

| Property | Examples | Result | Counterexample |
|---|---|---|---|
| 10.2 at most one effect | 60 | PASS | — |
| 10.3 rules hold under concurrency | 60 | FAIL (expectedFailure) | `steps=['assistant','assistant'], entitlement=412, jump=0` → total paid 824 > 412 |
| 10.4 receipts never overclaim | 60 | PASS | — |

**10.2** — For crash none/before/after × duplicate deliveries 0–3 × extra `recover()` 0–3 × clock jumps, each effect id yields at most one refund in the rail and at most one `COMMITTED` journal entry, and the receipt still verifies. Passes.

**10.3** — Hypothesis found a genuine counterexample: two assistant reissues for the same `(case, period)` both reach the rail (824 > 412). Root cause: `at_most_one_reissue` counts prior reissues from the **ledger** only (`CivicOffice.capture` → `ledger.reissues_for`), but a committed assistant reissue goes to the rail and is **not** written to the ledger in this pre-action spec (`PaymentRail.apply` touches only `backend.refunds`; ledger recording is Spec 2). So the second assistant reissue sees `prior_reissues == 0` and sends. The hand/assistant mix is correctly bounded; assistant/assistant is not. The fix (count prior reissues from the rail too) lives in `office.py`/`payment_rail.py`, which this task is scoped not to touch — so I did **not** weaken the property. I recorded the shrunk counterexample in a comment, marked the test `@unittest.expectedFailure`, and left task 10.3 unchecked in `tasks.md`.

**10.4** — For refused-by-rule (over-entitlement, duplicate, ineligible) and stale-premise (closed mid-decision) histories, `executed` is never `True` and no refund reaches the rail. An earlier draft wrongly included a crash-before + `recover()` case; Hypothesis falsified it (`kind='crash_before'` → `executed=True`). That is correct behavior, not an overclaim — recovery re-sends a tier-1 effect idempotently and the refund genuinely lands — so I removed it from the unconfirmed-history set and documented why. Passes.

Files touched: `tests/test_civic_properties.py` (new), `pyproject.toml` (dev dep), `.kiro/specs/civic-case-actions/tasks.md` (ticked 10.1/10.2/10.4, annotated 10.3). Temp files were written to `$env:TEMP` only. Ticked 10.x only for passing properties.
